WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 99:59:59.999
Phase 7, LLMs and generative AI. A language model is not magic; it is next-token prediction at scale, wrapped in tokenization, attention, and sampling. Understand the machinery and the failure modes before building a product on top of it. This phase has 3 lessons. Lesson 1: Tokens, embeddings, and context. Text becomes token IDs, token IDs become vectors, and the context window limits how much the model can attend to. Token counts drive cost and truncation. The question for this lesson: What does the model actually see when you send text?. A tokenizer splits text into subword pieces. Common words may be one token; rare words, code, and some languages get split into many. The model never sees your characters directly, only IDs and positions. Embeddings turn those IDs into vectors that shift with context. Similar meanings tend to land near each other, but the geometry is learned, not a philosophical map of truth. Count tokens before you build a prompt budget. You will learn: Tokenization is model-specific and not the same as words., Embeddings are learned vectors, not guaranteed semantic truth., Context limits apply to input plus output, including retrieved chunks.. Practice: "Tokenize the same paragraph in English. Watch out for: Assuming one word equals one token., Treating cosine similarity as proof of meaning., Forgetting retrieved context consumes the window.. You are ready when: Measure token counts., Test truncation., Budget input and output together.. Lesson 2: Fine-tuning and adaptation. Fine-tuning teaches behavior and format; retrieval supplies facts. LoRA makes adaptation cheaper, but data quality and evaluation still decide the result. The question for this lesson: When should you change the weights instead of the prompt?. Prompting changes the instruction. Retrieval changes the context. Fine-tuning changes the model's weights, which is a much heavier commitment. Use it when you need a consistent behavior, style, or task format that prompting cannot reliably produce. Parameter-efficient methods such as LoRA train small adapters instead of every weight. That lowers cost, not the need for clean examples, a held-out eval, and a prompt-only baseline to beat. You will learn: Fine-tune behavior and format; retrieve facts., LoRA adapters train a small number of added parameters., A fine-tuned model needs the same eval set as the baseline.. Practice: "Curate 200 clean examples. Watch out for: Fine-tuning before establishing a prompt baseline., Letting evaluation examples leak into training., Assuming more epochs always improve behavior.. You are ready when: Write the target behavior., Hold out evals., Compare cost and quality.. Lesson 3: Decoding, prompting, and structured output. Sampling settings control randomness and diversity. Structured output needs schema validation, and prompts are an interface that must be tested, versioned, and defended against injection. The question for this lesson: Why does the same prompt produce different answers?. A language model outputs a probability distribution over the next token. Greedy decoding takes the top choice; temperature and top-p sample from the rest. That is why the same prompt can produce different answers and why temperature zero is not a mathematical guarantee of determinism. When another program consumes the output, natural language is not enough. Ask for JSON against a schema, validate it, and handle the invalid case. Treat retrieved text and user text as untrusted input, not as instructions. You will learn: Temperature and top-p trade consistency for diversity., Structured output needs schema validation, not hope., Prompt injection is an input-trust problem, not just a wording problem.. Practice: "Run one prompt at temperature 0. Watch out for: Assuming temperature 0 means identical output forever., Parsing JSON without validating it., Putting untrusted retrieved text in the system instruction.. You are ready when: Version prompts., Validate structured output., Test injection cases.. You can explain how an LLM turns text into tokens and tokens into a response. Take your time with each lesson and practice the examples.
